%I A124140 #21 Oct 04 2026 09:51:41
%S A124140 24,78,90,224,260,264,348,570,728,846,996,1580,1692,2030,2514,3552,
%T A124140 3762,4560,4648,4668,5250,5976,6448,6650,6954,8634,10458,11994,13624,
%U A124140 14056,14476,16056,17064,17292,17412,18960,21712,22652,23500,24108,26940,27132,27412
%N A124140 Numbers k such that there is a bigger number m satisfying A000203(k) = A000203(m) = m + k - gcd(m,k).
%C A124140 R. Mathar computed this sequence up to 15000.
%H A124140 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A124140/b124140.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..500</a>
%H A124140 Max Alekseyev, <a href="https://oeis.org/wiki/User:Max_Alekseyev/gpscripts">PARI/GP Scripts for Miscellaneous Math Problems</a> (invphi.gp).
%e A124140 24 is in the sequence because A000203(24) = A000203(38) = 60 = 24 + 38 - gcd(24,38).
%o A124140 (PARI) isok(k) = {my(s = sigma(k), v = invsigma(s)); for(i = 1, #v, if(v[i] > k && s == v[i] + k - gcd(v[i], k), return(1))); 0; } \\ _Amiram Eldar_, Oct 04 2026, using _Max Alekseyev_'s invphi.gp
%Y A124140 Cf. A000203, A124141.
